module gunbc.plans.fabric_market

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.markdown_helpers { h2, p, li, ul, ol, quote }

fn fabric_market_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**This plan is written as an intention** (`gunbc.design_argument::plans_are_intentions`): what the repository should become, why, and done-when. How it is realized is downstream: the work items that serve it name it in `gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_intention_binding`, and this plan does not list them."),
      p(text: "**Grounding:** `gunbc.design_argument::interface_realization_policy`, `gunbc.design_argument::selection_precedes_convergence`, `gunbc.design_argument::external_upstreams`, `gunbc.design_argument::single_authority`, `gunbc.design_argument::replacement_migration`, `gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization`, `gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed`."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "1. Intention"),
    p(text: "**What:** every place the repository matches work with the resources that run it -- serving turns and sessions, remote and local builds, CI, the belt's attempts, rented and customer capacity, and applications not yet written -- is one market, designed from first principles in four layers (units, connection, market, venue) and named in the literal vocabulary of trading, grounded in the FIX protocol. Any executor, including an off-the-shelf scheduler such as Kubernetes with Kueue, is a venue that realizes our contract under written terms, and can be replaced by our own realization one term at a time."),
    p(text: "**Why (operator, 2026-11-23):** routing grew route by route. A survey of the tree on 2026-11-23 (section 4) found two choosing engines, host picks outside both (a roster walk, a Lua router, list-order and roster-order tie-breaks), five lease and fence identities, four attempt records, about twenty-one local outcome-unknown arms, three ledgers per host, two work identities, and no common contract for what an executor accepts and reports. The operator's policy goals -- prefer faster owned hosts, move a session when the throughput gain beats the cost of moving it, drain and move -- have a modeled home with no inputs and no caller, and a new application would today add its own scoring and fallback. One market with one vocabulary removes the cost of reconciling these by hand, and lets a new application enter by declaring what it trades rather than by writing a new scheduler."),
    p(text: "**Done when.** Each condition names the evidence that settles it: **[tree]** a fact of declarations, call paths and deletions on main; **[executed]** a control run on the real route that goes red when the rule is removed (its declaration alone is not the evidence); **[deployed]** a read-back of what is running; **[judgement]** the operator's."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**One matching engine, both directions.** [tree, executed] Every production route that chooses supply for an order, or an order for supply, reaches one matching engine built on `std.decision`. It returns the whole attributed outcome -- order identity and decision scope on every arm (selected, needs evidence, needs policy, refused, no feasible candidate, field unestablished) -- and a successful selection is distinct from the admission that may still refuse it. No roster walk, router-local score, list order or roster-order tie-break chooses outside it, except as a term delegated to a venue and written down as such."),
      li(text: "**Speed is a term.** [executed] A candidate's run time (or throughput) for the work in question is a reading on the candidate, conditioned on the workload, the admitted configuration, the retained state and the concurrent load, with its evidence standing. Under a declared objective with the other funded terms equal, a faster host wins on it; an unread run time is unknown, never zero."),
      li(text: "**The vocabulary is grounded, mapped and single.** [tree] Order, side, limit price, time in force, order status, execution type, fill, execution report, cancel, cancel-replace, cancel reject, status request and quote are taken from a cited, versioned `extdeps` model of FIX; the market layer states what each means for execution resources (section 2); order book, matching engine, broker, market maker, venue, clearing and settlement are roles with stated obligations. No second name survives: `BuyTerms`, `maximum_buy_order`, `SupplierOffer` and `contention` are replaced by atomic semantic cuts that keep every distinction they carried (liability ceiling, selection cost, actual spend; capabilities, route, availability, isolation, price evidence), not kept beside the new names."),
      li(text: "**Three clocks, declared per order.** [tree, executed] Every order states its time in force -- immediate-or-cancel, fill-or-kill, or good-till-date -- which bounds only how long unmatched quantity may seek a fill. Grant validity and occupancy, and the work's own timing, are separate: an order that expires or is cancelled leaves its earlier fills and their grants standing until they are settled. No plan or module says one route queues while its contract says it refuses."),
      li(text: "**Conservation is one authority.** [executed] A fill authorizes execution only after one admission commits every resource and money reservation it needs, or none. Overlapping commitments on a host, including unreleased occupancy, stay within its capacity; a delegated budget bounds its children, who neither manufacture capacity nor charge it twice; a parent's expiry does not free capacity while a child may still run; a relocation charges both sides until release is established. Every host consumer -- serving, sessions, builds, CI, the belt, the demand engine -- clears through it."),
      li(text: "**One connection layer.** [tree, executed] One work identity that excludes the resource estimate, with the objective on the order rather than the work; one fence representation over `std.durable_compare_and_set` that keeps each authority's own scope; one execution-attempt record that keeps the effect attempts it contains distinct; one carrier for delivery uncertainty, settled by readback and never by re-sending, with each domain's result and release obligation kept beside it."),
      li(text: "**One venue contract.** [tree, executed] Every executor -- the SSH remote build, the systemd compute provider, the serving router, a scheduler -- realizes one venue contract: it accepts a committed fill, never re-matches it, enforces the granted limits, and reports uncertainty, cancellation evidence and re-observation after restart. Each venue states which of four guarantees it provides -- one logical acceptance of a repeated submission, one physical execution, one authoritative published result, correct accounting while duplicates run -- and each external venue has a written list of delegated terms, each naming what we would build to take it back."),
      li(text: "**Serving.** [executed, deployed] The serving router is a native transport that realizes `gunbc.serving.router_realization::router_capability_permission`; sessions move only through the relocation operation. The hand-written Lua router is retired from the tree [tree], and a read-back shows the deployed route is the native one with the Lua path gone and nothing falling back to it [deployed]."),
      li(text: "**Completion covers every route.** [tree] The intention's terminal depends on the serving cutover, the work-execution program through one ledger per host, the belt's move onto fabric leases, and the demand engine's occupancy (`gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_intention_binding`), so the routes in the section 4 inventory all lie inside its dependency closure."),
      li(text: "**[judgement]** A new application (a customer workload, a rented GPU pool, a storage or network resource) enters by declaring its instrument and its orders, with no new scoring, fallback or ledger of its own; one representative additional instrument is the evidence offered for it."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "2. The four layers"),
    p(text: "The order is a dependency order: each layer states what must already be true for the one above it."),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "**Units.** What is traded and who trades it. A unit of work has a semantic identity, requirements and an outcome contract; its resource estimate is evidence about it, not part of its identity. A unit of supply is a holder of resources with capabilities, capacity and evidence. The thing traded is an **instrument**: a resource shape on a host class for a duration, a serving seat, a build cell, or whatever a new application declares, with its quantity, units and divisibility (an indivisible bundle -- a serving turn, a build's CPU and memory together -- trades in quantity one; a partial fill never means receiving only some of its dimensions)."),
      li(text: "**Connection.** How units are joined over time, and where conservation is made true: dependencies between units; admission, which commits every reservation a fill needs or none and decides between competing claims on the same residual capacity; grants, leases and delegated budgets, where a child spends its parent's budget; attempts and their lineage, with the effect attempts each contains; fencing; receipts; cancellation evidence; release and recovery. A lease that expires does not free capacity while its holder may still be running (`std.resource_release_law`)."),
      li(text: "**Market.** Orders, their objectives and time in force, eligibility, the matching policy, and the order-facing roles of clearing and settlement. A buy **order** carries a demand: its instrument and quantity, its objective (`product.fabric.demand::DemandObjective`; two orders for one computation may value delay differently without being different work) and its monetary terms; a sell order (an ask, or offer) carries supply, and standing owned capacity is a resting sell order. Orders rest in an **order book** at a **venue**, and a **matching engine** selects; admission turns a selection into a **fill** (possibly partial), a committed allocation linked to its grant, reported by an **execution report**. **Clearing** is the role that assumes each fill is backed by capacity, using the connection layer's admission; **settlement** discharges it, with the delivery read back, capacity released after quiescence and the money settled. A **broker** routes a client's orders to venues under a best-execution duty measured against the client's objective; a **market maker** quotes both sides, buying and selling capacity. A **drain** halts new admission on a venue and relocates or terminates the work already there; a **recall** requests preemption, with termination and release established separately; and relocation is closing a position on one venue and opening it on another when the gain exceeds the switching cost. These are roles, not components: any program may play any of them."),
      li(text: "**Venue.** What an executor must accept from the market and report back: realizing a committed fill, enforcing its scope and limits, reporting observations, and placing within a granted pool only as a delegated term. An order resting in the book is market state; granted work awaiting placement inside a pool is venue state. Kubernetes with Kueue, the SSH remote build, the systemd provider and the serving router are venues."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**The product mapping of FIX.** FIX defines order and execution messages for financial instruments; this market trades execution resources, so each term carries a stated product meaning:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Order:** a request to acquire a quantity of an identified instrument under declared terms. **Selection:** an attributable decision about an eligible candidate; admission can still refuse it. **Fill:** a committed allocation of instrument quantity, linked to its grant and obligations. **Order status** and **execution type:** facts about the order and its allocation events, kept apart as FIX keeps `OrdStatus` and `ExecType` apart. **Lease or fence:** the authority to use what was allocated, within a scope and generation. **Work attempt and outcome:** the computation itself and its observed result. **Settlement and release:** the evidence-backed disposition of the obligations and reservations."),
      li(text: "So a capacity order can be filled while its computation is still running, and that computation can still fail; a cancel removes unmatched quantity while fills already made stay real; a fill can arrive while a cancel is pending."),
      li(text: "**Money.** The current contract's four distinctions survive the rename: the liability reserved before granting, the cost used to compare candidates (including valued delay), the spend settled afterwards, and the liability ceiling for one grant. A limit price per unit does not by itself preserve a total ceiling, so the submitted buy order carries its price and budget terms -- quantity, price basis, billing quantum and liability ceiling -- while the encumbrance, the candidate valuation and the settled spend are linked facts produced by their own authorities (admission, matching and settlement)."),
      li(text: "**Matching policy.** FIX does not prescribe a universal matching algorithm. This market's policy weighs several terms -- money, delay, run time, the cost of moving, interruption -- under the order's objective, as `std.decision` over `std.pareto` axes; single-price matching is its special case. Stated on the market layer (`gunbc.design_argument::conformance`)."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "3. Rulings this intention rests on"),
    p(text: "Each is recorded, with the operator's words, in `gunbc.design_ruling`; this plan states what follows from it and does not restate the ruling."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "`gunbc.design_ruling::execution_is_one_first_principles_market_and_schedulers_are_venues`: the four layers are designed from first principles end to end, current integrations are realizations to be replaced, and an external scheduler is a venue conforming to our contract under clear terms that our own implementation can take back. It restates the work-execution-redesign program's delegation of node placement: the scheduler places within a granted pool, as a written term."),
      li(text: "`gunbc.design_ruling::market_vocabulary_is_fix_and_matching_is_std_decision`: the literal trading vocabulary, grounded in FIX; roles not components; one matching engine on `std.decision`, with a per-candidate run-time term."),
      li(text: "`gunbc.design_ruling::live_router_changes_of_2026_11_23_are_requirements_evidence`: the router changes made by hand on 2026-11-23 are respected as evidence of what the market must express, and are not a design to build on."),
      li(text: "Earlier rulings this intention consumes: `gunbc.design_ruling::hosted_ci_bridges_to_remote_builds_and_adopts_work_execution_redesign` (the work-execution program and the remote-build bridge), `gunbc.design_ruling::remote_build_ssh_dispatch_is_an_admitted_scaffold`."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "4. Observation, 2026-11-23"),
    p(text: "A read-only survey in four parts, one per layer, by bounded grep and declaration reads under dag/ at 3ba2851be3db, with spot checks by hand at 416595d5b1ed. Production means a non-test importer; whether each route runs live was not established. A negative grep is not proof of absence."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Units.** `product.fabric.work::derive_work_key` hashes the resource shape into the work identity (the work-exec-0 defect, still present), while `gunbc.compute.work_provider_local::compute_work_identity` is a second work identity without it. Threads are stated twice (`HardRequirements.threads`, `ResourceEnvelope.cpu.min_threads`). Each host's capacity sits in at least three ledgers: `gunbc.compute.host_capacity::HostCapacityPool`, `gunbc.serving.serving_reservation::serving_host_reservations`, and the runner-slot widths. `gunbc.compute.work_class_grant::WorkloadProfile` is already an estimate carrier with evidence standing. Nothing converts a `WorkOperation` into a `product.fabric.work::Work`, so builds never enter the market."),
      li(text: "**Connection.** Lease and fence identity exists five ways (`std.temporal_effect::LeaseEpoch`, `product.fabric.execution::LeaseIdentity`, `product.capacity.lease::LeaseFence`, `gunbc.fabric.fabric_control_plane::CellReservation` generations, `std.durable_exclusive_hold` generations); the attempt four ways (fabric `Attempt`, compute `AttemptRecord` and `ControllerAttempt`, the belt's `roadmap_attempt_*`, `std.temporal_effect::EffectAttemptIntent`). About twenty-one local arms each spell an unknown outcome, with no shared carrier and no shared readback rule. No dependency edges join separate works. The plan carrier the work-execution nodes named, `dag/gunbc/plans/durable_work_handoff.dag`, was deleted in #16107; this plan is now their carrier."),
      li(text: "**Market.** `product.fabric.selection::select_supply` takes the lowest total cost over one duration for every candidate, so a faster host cannot win on run time, and it does not consume `std.decision`; `gunbc.serving.serving_relocation`, `gunbc.serving.serving_replica_choice` and `product.fabric.flex_market` do, so two decision engines exist. `select_resource_budget_supply` takes the first eligible entry of its list. `product.fabric.contention::select_demand_for_offer` and `serving_relocation::decide_relocation` have no production importer. Owned serving replicas have no route to an admitted `CandidateOffer`, while workspace and microVM runners do, through `product.fabric.candidate_field::assemble_candidate_field`. Host picks outside the core: `gunbc.compute.remote_build` roster order, `gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy`, roster-order tie-breaks in `gunbc.fabric.fabric_control_plane` and `gunbc.harness.harness_seat`. The existing nodes fabric-demand-objective, fabric-consequence-standing, fabric-dimension-extension-law, fabric-scenario-evaluation and fabric-customer-ci-failover already own the objective and the evidence standing; none owns a run-time term or the two-engine split."),
      li(text: "**Venue.** No common venue interface: `gunbc.compute.remote_build` reports a string receipt and walks a roster; `gunbc.compute.work_provider_local::compute_provide` reports a typed `WorkOutcome` and is also a capacity ledger; the belt's `roadmap_belt_actuate` and `attempt_system_unit_launch` keep their own vocabularies; `gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy` breaks all five forbidden arms of `router_capability_permission`. `product.fabric.execution` (`ExecutionGrant`, `Receipt`, `admit_receipt_for_grant`) is a modeled venue contract that no live route consumes. There is no `extdeps` model for Kubernetes, Kueue or Nomad; systemd and cgroup v2 are modeled. `gunbc.compute.remote_build` says both that the scheduler work-exec-1 chooses will select hosts and that work-exec-2's `select_supply` will; the first ruling in section 3 resolves this: the market fills, the scheduler places within the fill."),
      li(text: "**Plans that overlap this one.** `gunbc.plans.fabric_serving_router` holds the serving router and relocation stages and their acceptance as prose, says excess turns queue in turn_admission while `gunbc.serving.caller_retry_contract` refuses, and has no roadmap items. The work-execution-redesign program is held as roadmap rows. `gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program` M4 and M5 place work inside one evaluation and must consume the host's clearing ledger rather than allocate beside it."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**Route inventory.** Each route the survey found that chooses or allocates, with today's authorities and its disposition. Bounded by the survey above: it is a candidate list, not a census, and a route found later joins it."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Remote build** (`gunbc.compute.remote_build`, the `remote-build` client): chooses by roster order; allocates in each host's `ComputeBuildPool`. Disposition: matching moves to the engine and allocation to admission; the SSH transport dissolves into the durable route."),
      li(text: "**CI verdict** (the required job driving remote builds): chooses through the remote build. Disposition: the verdict is read from an admitted receipt."),
      li(text: "**Local compute provider** (`gunbc.compute.work_provider_local::compute_provide`): does not choose; allocates in `ComputeBuildPool` itself. Disposition: first realization of the venue contract; allocation moves to admission."),
      li(text: "**Belt attempts** (`gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_belt_actuate`): does not choose; keeps its own attempt, lease and slot state. Disposition: onto the fabric attempt record and leases."),
      li(text: "**Workspace and microVM runners** (`gunbc.fabric.fabric_control_plane::broker_select_for_requirements`, `gunbc.runner.runner_microvm_slot_reserve`): choose through `select_supply` with a roster-order tie-break; allocate through `CellReservation`. Disposition: the engine, with the tie-break declared or deleted."),
      li(text: "**Harness seats** (`gunbc.harness.harness_seat`): choose through `select_supply` with a roster-order tie-break; allocate through the seat ledger. Disposition: the engine; seats clear through admission."),
      li(text: "**Floor dispatch** (`gunbc.fabric.fabric_floor_dispatch`): selects and reserves nothing; GitHub places. Disposition: the engine; execution through the CI route."),
      li(text: "**Rented capacity** (`product.fabric.flex_market`): its own ranking through `std.decision`. Disposition: onto the engine."),
      li(text: "**Demand-side matching** (`product.fabric.contention::select_demand_for_offer`): no production importer. Disposition: folded into the engine if resting orders need it, otherwise deleted."),
      li(text: "**Serving** (`gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy`, the live Lua router): chooses, binds and moves sessions itself; allocation in its own tables. Disposition: native transport, relocation, cutover."),
      li(text: "**Demand-engine occupancy** (`gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program` M5): child processes and jobserver tokens inside one evaluation, grant home open (D7). Disposition: a child of the parent-grant contract."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "5. Requirements evidence: the router as changed by hand on 2026-11-23"),
    p(text: "Reported to the operator from the live router configuration (`~/router/nginx.conf` on the router host, with backups `~/router/*.bak-before-efficiency-tiers-*`); not read back by this session, and not recorded anywhere in the repository before this plan. Per `gunbc.design_ruling::live_router_changes_of_2026_11_23_are_requirements_evidence`, each is a requirement the market must be able to express, not a mechanism to keep:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Efficiency tiers** (tier 0 and tier 1): engines are ranked, and a request goes to the better tier first. Market reading: a run-time or throughput term on the candidate, under the demand's objective."),
      li(text: "**Upward-only moves:** the router moves a session only toward a better tier. Market reading: relocation selected only when the gain beats the switching cost, with stay preferred on a tie (`gunbc.serving.serving_relocation`)."),
      li(text: "**Live retain:** a row retains 8 conversations counting live sessions, and srv4 retains 1. Market reading: retained state is a reservation with a growth allowance, cleared against the host's ledger."),
      li(text: "**A router-side binding table.** Market reading: session attachment belongs to its lifecycle owner, not to the transport."),
      li(text: "**A ruling carried only in router comments**, dated 2026-10-27: congestion never moves a bound session, on vLLM too. It stands beside the recorded 2026-10-28 ruling in `gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy` (one placement per conversation; deliberate moves only through the relocation operation) and is superseded where the 2026-11-23 upward-only moves contradict it; the cutover gives each a disposition."),
    ]),
    p(text: "The cutover owes a read-back of the live file before it deletes it, so this list is checked against what actually ran."),
    h2(text: "6. Realization"),
    p(text: "The work that converges on section 1 is not authored here. Work items name this intention in `gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_intention_binding` and may be redrawn without this plan changing. The existing work-execution, belt and demand-engine nodes keep their own bindings and owners; this intention reaches them through dependency edges -- where they consume its common contracts, and from its terminal, which is the market's completion coverage over them. The work-execution nodes also carry this plan as their design carrier, and their program text is restated by the first ruling in section 3."),
    h2(text: "7. Open questions"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Which FIX version** the `extdeps` model cites (FIX Latest, or a numbered release), and which subset it models first: the order and execution messages are needed; market data and allocation may not be."),
      li(text: "**The product word.** `gunbc.product_names` anchors `fabric` on `product.fabric.selection::select_supply` as compute placement. Whether `fabric` names the whole market and the matching engine is one role inside it, or the anchor moves, is decided when the market module lands."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data fabric_market_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly,
  slug: "fabric-market",
  title: "Fabric market: units, connection, market and venue as one first-principles design in trading terms",
  body: fabric_market_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Section 1 is achieved: every done-when condition holds on the tree, or for the judgement condition the operator has accepted it, and this plan has been deleted or rewritten as a typed intention.") },
}
